  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1026 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nWARM AND DRY WITH ISOLATED MOUNTAIN STORMS THROUGH TOMORROW:\n\nHigh pressure is centered over the Four Corners area and will\nremain centered there for the next 24 hours or so. Tomorrow the\nhigh pressure will break down as a shortwave trough propagates \nacross the Intermountain West, moving across our area on \nTuesday. Generally warm and dry conditions are expected today \nand tomorrow while high pressure remains aloft. High \ntemperatures will continue to be 5 to 15 degrees above normal \nwith the most anomalous temperatures occurring in the northern \nhalf of the CWA. Lingering moisture circulating within the area \nof high pressure will allow for isolated afternoon convection \nover the high terrain, primarily over the San Juans. \n\nPATTERN CHANGE ON TUESDAY: \n\nOn Tuesday a shortwave trough will move across our area,\nproviding support for increased precipitation chances, mostly\nover the higher terrain. However, forcing and moisture still do\nnot look substantial enough to support significant rainfall.\nAbove normal high temperatures and afternoon mountain\nthunderstorms are forecast each day though the end of the \nworkweek.",
